---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const REPO = 'https://github.com/markramm/make-yourself-expensive';
const DATA_REPO = 'https://github.com/markramm/data-broker-registry';

// Prefilled issue links. Deliberately NOT a form on this site: there is no server here,
// so a form would either need one (breaking the whole premise) or quietly lose the report.
// A prefilled GitHub link keeps the no-server guarantee and leaves the words in the
// tester's own hands -- they see and edit everything before it is sent.
const brokerBody = `**Which broker?**
(name or domain, e.g. spokeo.com)

**What did the tool tell you to expect?**


**What actually happened?**
(different fields? unexpected CAPTCHA? page missing? account required?)

**Did the listing come down?**
(not yet / yes, after ___ days / no)

**Browser and device**
(e.g. Firefox 141 on macOS, Safari on iPhone)

---
Please do not paste your real name, address, or profile contents here.
Describe the shape of the problem, not your personal data.`;

const siteBody = `**Where in the site?**
(page URL or section)

**What happened?**


**What did you expect instead?**


**Browser and device**


---
Please do not paste your real name, address, or profile contents here.`;

const confusingBody = `**What did you read?**
(page and roughly where on it)

**Where did you get lost?**


**What would have helped?**


---
This is the most valuable report from a first-time reader, and it expires:
once you have used the tool, you cannot see it fresh again.`;

const u = (title: string, body: string, labels: string) =>
  `${REPO}/issues/new?title=${encodeURIComponent(title)}&body=${encodeURIComponent(body)}&labels=${encodeURIComponent(labels)}`;

const brokerUrl = u('Broker report: ', brokerBody, 'tester-report,broker');
const siteUrl = u('Site bug: ', siteBody, 'tester-report,bug');
const confusingUrl = u('Confusing: ', confusingBody, 'tester-report,clarity');
---

<BaseLayout title="Testing this site">
  <h1>Testing this site</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    This is an <strong>alpha</strong>. It works; it is not finished. The fastest way to make
    it better is someone who did not build it trying to actually opt out of something with it.
  </p>

  <p>
    You do not need to be technical. If you can follow instructions and say "this step didn't
    work," that is the whole job.
  </p>

  <h2>Before you start</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>Nothing you type into this site leaves your browser.</strong> No server receives
    your name, address, or progress — it is a static page, and everything is stored locally on
    your device. You can <a href={REPO}>read the code</a>, or have someone technical you trust
    read it. The one exception is an aggregate, cookie-free page-view count, which never
    carries anything from your profile and never records which broker you clicked.
  </p>

  <p class="callout">
    <strong>One caution.</strong> This is alpha software handling personal information. If your
    address being findable is a physical safety matter — you are an abuse survivor, you are
    being harassed, you hold public office — please don't learn this tool on your live data.
    Try it with a throwaway profile first, or wait for a later release.
  </p>

  <h2>The three things most worth testing</h2>

  <h3>1. Does an opt-out actually work, start to finish?</h3>
  <p>
    Pick <strong>one</strong> broker and follow it the whole way. This is the highest-value
    report by a wide margin — it is the one thing the author cannot do at scale alone. Did the
    broker's page look like what we said? Were the fields we listed the fields it asked for?
    Was there a CAPTCHA we didn't warn you about? <strong>Did your listing actually come
    down, and how long did it take?</strong>
  </p>

  <h3>2. Is the instruction wrong, or just hard?</h3>
  <p>
    Those are different bugs and we want them apart. <em>"The page said to paste a listing URL
    but there's no field for it"</em> means the instruction is wrong. <em>"I couldn't figure
    out where to find my listing URL"</em> means it is unclear. Both matter; the second is
    more common and gets under-reported because people assume it's their fault. It isn't.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Does it make sense before you've done it once?</h3>
  <p>
    Read the home page and the top of the broker list as though you'd never heard of any of
    this. Where do you get lost? Which word did you have to look up? The author has stared at
    this for months and can't see it fresh anymore. You can — exactly once. Spend it.
  </p>

  <h2>File a report</h2>

  <p>
    These open a pre-filled GitHub issue. You'll see everything before it's sent, and you can
    edit or delete any of it. A GitHub account is required — if that's a barrier, reply to the
    post that brought you here instead.
  </p>

  <ul class="report-links">
    <li><a href={brokerUrl}>A broker's instructions were wrong or incomplete →</a></li>
    <li><a href={siteUrl}>Something on the site is broken →</a></li>
    <li><a href={confusingUrl}>Something was confusing or unclear →</a></li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <strong>Please don't paste your real name, address, or profile contents into an issue.</strong>
    Describe the shape of the problem, not your personal data. Issues are public.
  </p>

  <h2>Known-incomplete — no need to report these</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Most entries are marked UNVERIFIED</strong> — 456 of 493 right now. That badge is
      honest, not broken: it means no human has yet confirmed the routing against the broker's
      own page. Finding that an unverified entry is <em>wrong</em> is exactly the contribution
      we want. The badge tells you where to look.
    </li>
    <li><strong>The <a href="/harden/">Harden</a> section is thin.</strong> Device guides are next, not done.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Some brokers are dead.</strong> Domains lapse constantly in this industry.
      Telling us a site no longer exists is a real fix — we drop them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No sync between devices.</strong> By design. Progress lives in one browser on one
      machine, and clearing site data clears it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>If the bug is in the broker data itself</h2>

  <p>
    Wrong URL, wrong fields, or a dead site belongs in the dataset repo rather than this one:
    <a href={DATA_REPO}>data-broker-registry</a>. One markdown file per broker, no code
    required — see its <code>CONTRIBUTING.md</code>. The broker-report link above files to the
    app repo, which is fine; we move them.
  </p>

  <h2>Why this exists</h2>

  <p>
    A 2024 <em>Consumer Reports</em> study found paid removal services took down about
    <strong>35%</strong> of participants' records over four months. Doing it by hand removed
    <strong>70%</strong>. Free DIY matches the best paid service and beats the rest — so the
    only thing standing between most people and that 70% is an hour and knowing where to
    start. This site is the "where to start," and testing is what makes it trustworthy enough
    to hand to someone else.
  </p>

  <style>
    .lede { font-size: 1.1rem; }
    .callout {
      border-left: 3px solid currentColor;
      padding-left: 1rem;
      opacity: 0.9;
    }
    .report-links { line-height: 2; }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
